<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-simple-algebras" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Simple algebras</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
        We introduce <sq>simple algebras</sq> in this section. Although the term <sq>semisimple algebra</sq>, introduced earlier, suggests that it is a generalization of simple algebra, it is not true without some additional conditions.
    </p>
    <p>
        We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <definition xml:id="def-simple-algebra">
    <title>Simple algebra</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An <m>R</m>-algebra <m>A</m> is said to be <em>simple</em> if its only two-sided ideals are <m>\{0\}</m> and <m>A</m> itself.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <title>Quaternion algebra is simple over a field of characteristic not <m>2</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            We have shown in <xref ref="prop-quaternion-algebra-is-simple"/> that the quaternion algebra <m>(a,b)_F</m> over a field of characteristic not equal to <m>2</m> is a simple algebra.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  <p>
    In the next propostion we characterize simple algebras that are semisimple.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-simple-iff-semisimple-artinian">
    <statement>
        <p>
            For a simple algebra <m>A</m>, following are equivalent:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is semisimple;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> has a minimal right ideal.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <proof>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>A</m> is a simple algebra as well as a semisimple algebra. We show that <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian. Note that for any nonzero <m>a\in A</m>, the two-sided ideal <m>AaA</m> is nonzero and hence, <m>AaA=A</m>. Therefore, there are <m>x_i,y_i\in A</m> such that <m>1=\sum_{i=1}^n x_iay_i</m>. This implies that <m>A_A</m> is generated by <m>\{x_1a,x_2a,\ldots,x_na\}</m>, i.e., <m>A_A</m> is finitely generated. In view of <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/>, <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian. This shows that (1) implies (2).
    </p>
    <p>
        If <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian then, by <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, <m>A_A</m> has a minimal right ideal. This shows that (2) implies (3).
    </p>
    <p>
        Now assume that <m>N</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>. Hence, <m>N\neq 0</m> and <m>AN</m>, a two-sided ideal of <m>A</m> generated by <m>N</m>, is <m>A</m>. Thus, <me>
            A=\sum_{a\in A}aN.
        </me>
        Note that each <m>aN</m> is a right ideal of <m>A</m>, i.e., each <m>aN</m> is an <m>A</m>-module. Furthermore, each <m>aN\simeq N</m> as an <m>A</m>-modules. Hence, <m>A</m> is written as a sum of simple <m>A</m>-modules, i.e., <m>A</m> is a semisimple algebra. This proves that (3) implies (1).
    </p>
  </proof>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-semisimple-simple-iff-minimal-right-ideals-are-isomorphic">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Following statements are equivalent for a semisimple algebra <m>A</m>:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is simple;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        any two minimal right ideals of <m>A</m> are isomorphic;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        any two simple <m>A</m>-modules are isomorphic.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <proof>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>A</m> is simple. Let <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> are minimal right ideals of <m>A</m>. Thus, <m>AM</m> and <m>AN</m> are nonzero ideals of <m>A</m>. As <m>A</m> is simple, we have <m>AM=A=AN</m>. Therefore,
        <me>
            A(MN)=(AM)N=AN=A.
        </me>
        In particular, <m>MN\neq 0</m>. By <xref ref="prop-equivalent-conditions-on-minimal-ideals-of-semisimple-algebras"/>, <m>M\simeq N</m>. This shows that (1) implies (2).
    </p>
    <p>
        In view of <xref ref="prop-modules-over-semisimple-algebra"/>, (2) and (3) are equivalent.
    </p>
    <p>
        Assume now that any two minimal right ideals of <m>A</m> are isomorphic. We show that <m>A</m> is simple. Let <m>I</m> be a nonzero proper two-sided ideal of <m>A</m>. As <m>A</m> is semisimple, so is <m>I</m> as an <m>A</m>-module. Hence, there is a simple <m>A</m>-submodule <m>N\subseteq I</m>. Note that by <xref ref="prop-modules-over-semisimple-algebra"/>, <m>N</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>. By the assumption, any two minimal right ideals are isomorphic. Hence, for a minimal right ideal <m>J</m> there exists <m>x\in A</m> with <m>J=xN</m> (see <xref ref="prop-equivalent-conditions-on-minimal-ideals-of-semisimple-algebras"/>). Therefore, <m>A=\sum_{x\in A} xN\subseteq I</m>. This proves (2) implies (1).
    </p>
  </proof>
  <p>
    We require the following propostion regarding uniqueness of direct sum representation of semisimple modules.
  </p>
  <p>
    Suppose that <m>\left\{N_i:i\in I\right\}</m> be a set of representatives of the isomorphism classes of simple <m>A</m>-modules. We denote by <m>M(i)=\sum\left\{N&lt;M:N\simeq N_i\right\}</m> the submodule of a right <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m>.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-uniqueness-of-direct-sum-in-semisimple-module">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>M</m> and <m>M'</m> are semisimple right <m>A</m>-modules. Suppose that <m>M=\oplus_{i\in I}M(i)</m> with <m>M(i)=\oplus_{\alpha_i}N_i</m> and that <m>M'=\oplus_{i\in I}M'(i)</m> with <m>M'(i)=\oplus_{\beta_i}N_i</m>. Then, <m>M\simeq M'</m> if and only if the cardinal numbers <m>\alpha_i</m> and <m>\beta_i</m> are equal for all <m>i\in I</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <corollary xml:id="cor-module-decomposition-over-simple-algebra">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be a simple algebra and let <m>N</m> be a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>. If <m>M</m> is a right <m>A</m>-module then there exists a unique cardinal number <m>\alpha</m> such that <m>M\simeq\oplus_{\alpha}N</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </corollary>
  <proof>
    <p>
        As simple algebra <m>A</m> has a minimal right ideal, <m>A</m> is semisimple by <xref ref="prop-simple-iff-semisimple-artinian"/>. So, as a right module over a semisimple algebra, <m>M</m> is semisimple module (see <xref ref="prop-modules-over-semisimple-algebra"/>). Therefore, <m>M</m> is a direct sum of simple <m>A</m>-modules. However, <xref ref="prop-semisimple-simple-iff-minimal-right-ideals-are-isomorphic"/> implies that any two simple <m>A</m>-modules are isomorphic, i.e., <m>M\simeq\oplus_{\alpha}N</m>. The uniqueness of <m>\alpha</m> follows from <xref ref="prop-uniqueness-of-direct-sum-in-semisimple-module"/>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <corollary xml:id="cor-modules-over-finite-dim-simple-algebra">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>F</m> be a field and <m>A</m> be a finite-dimensional simple <m>F</m>-algbera. Suppose that <m>M_1</m> and <m>M_2</m> are right <m>A</m>-modules. Then, <m>M_1\simeq M_2</m> if and only if <m>\dim_FM_1=\dim_FM_2</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </corollary>
  <proof>
    <p>
        As <m>A</m> is an <m>F</m>-algebra and <m>M_i</m> are <m>A</m>-modules, we can consider <m>M_i</m> as an <m>F</m>-modules (see <xref ref="def-change-of-scalars"/>), i.e., <m>M_i</m> are <m>F</m>-vector spaces. As <m>A</m> is finite-dimensional vector space over <m>F</m>, it is both Artinian and Noetherian, and thus <m>A</m> is semisimple (see <xref ref="prop-simple-iff-semisimple-artinian"/>). By <xref ref="prop-modules-over-semisimple-algebra"/>, <m>M_i</m> are semisimple <m>A</m>-modules. By <xref ref="cor-module-decomposition-over-simple-algebra"/>, <m>M_i\simeq\oplus{\alpha_i}N</m> for unique cardinal numbers <m>\alpha_i</m> and a minimal right ideal <m>N</m> of <m>A</m>. By <xref ref="prop-uniqueness-of-direct-sum-in-semisimple-module"/>, <m>M_1\simeq M_2</m> if and only if <m>\alpha_1=\alpha_2</m>. As <m>\dim_FN&lt;\infty</m>, <m>\alpha_1=\alpha_2</m> if and only <m>\dim_FM_1=\dim_FM_2</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>